209 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 88 0 R
/E (Everything listed in this program is a work set. Take however many warm-up sets you need to find your working weight before your first work set. For the main lifts, you will likely need 2 to 4 warm-up sets ramping up in weight. For accessory exercises, 1 to 2 warm-up sets is usually enough. Warm-up sets do not count toward your total.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
210 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 88 0 R
/E (For your work sets, use reps in reserve as your guide to intensity.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
211 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 88 0 R
/E (Reps in reserve, or RIR, refers to how many reps you could still perform before reaching failure. Leaving 2 to 4 reps in reserve means the set is challenging but not a grind. You finish the set feeling like you had 2 to 4 more reps left in the tank if you needed them.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
212 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 88 0 R
/E (In the first few weeks of this program, aim for 2 to 4 RIR on all work sets. This allows you to learn the movements, find your working weights, and build consistency without accumulating excessive fatigue.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
213 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 88 0 R
/E (Over time, as you become more comfortable with the program and exercises, work toward 1-3 RIR. The sets get harder. The stimulus gets stronger. The results follow.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [10]
>>
